                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "8f53b81a-19e4-4e49-af48-2fd8c5d255e7",
                        "source_entry_id": "d7e9c8ad-052a-37ff-82c9-44190430207b",
                        "source_sense_id": "be6e5fae-77ea-3a94-9fde-b4891d8c47cd",
                        "source_sense_description": "Literatur: Geschichte, die in einer fiktiven, sich zum Negativen entwickelten Gesellschaft spielt",
                        "target_lang_word": "dystopia",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "dystopia",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "5c8c9436-d3b5-3653-9bbd-05539e579350",
                                        "entry_id": "adcda362-e596-3b26-a5ab-9071d666c8a8",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A miserable, dysfunctional state or society that has a very poor standard of living or severe censorship, oppression, etc."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "06c4f714-99df-4e00-b1b8-c54d3c2cd083",
                                                "sense_id": "5c8c9436-d3b5-3653-9bbd-05539e579350",
                                                "text": "As novelist, he knows, too, that when he sees the future, it will not work—he will automatically be creating a “dystopia” (no one creates utopias any more: even the utopias of the past look like dystopias to us)."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "eeacbc52-d80a-46da-b006-96868aeb8857",
                                                "sense_id": "5c8c9436-d3b5-3653-9bbd-05539e579350",
                                                "text": "2. FEAR OF TECHNOLOGY/THE BOMB/THE FUTURE—Progress run amok, either in the form of cybernetic creatures that turn against their masters, or future dystopiae in which society is controlled by technology."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d35fd77b-b221-487d-bf6a-ff98676ab9b1",
                                                "sense_id": "5c8c9436-d3b5-3653-9bbd-05539e579350",
                                                "text": "Erich Fromm, who has commented on 1984 and other dystopiae in postmodern literature discovers a mechanized dystopia in the text of existence itself in the ’50s and ’60s—a “technological nightmare” that had turned people into zombies and made the darkest alternative to “boring aliveness” seem attractive."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "99a942e2-e0e4-487b-a8eb-cb7e7c93299e",
                                                "sense_id": "5c8c9436-d3b5-3653-9bbd-05539e579350",
                                                "text": "[Aimee Semple] McPherson’s sexier half becomes the object of Jim [Morrison]’s obsession, and as the two struggle to find each other in this disordered land, their wild, careening chase through a dozen dystopiae recalls imagined worlds as diverse as Anthony Burgess’s A Clockwork Orange and Terry Gilliam’s movie Brazil."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "45857946-631e-40a6-8cd4-7163307a744a",
                                                "sense_id": "5c8c9436-d3b5-3653-9bbd-05539e579350",
                                                "text": "Recollections tattooed with stiletto reasons and a sorrowful slant of light admitting of dystopiae already petrified in progress."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "797620e1-098e-42ec-a682-70b1ee80ab99",
                                                "sense_id": "5c8c9436-d3b5-3653-9bbd-05539e579350",
                                                "text": "Its function is to legitimate that particular form of moral tribalism based on the “pedagogy of hatred” that [Evgeny] Zamiatin and [George] Orwell described so brilliantly in their dystopiae."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ec0d8418-f8b2-4a9e-82d2-42e440d6a291",
                                                "sense_id": "5c8c9436-d3b5-3653-9bbd-05539e579350",
                                                "text": "But you get to the beach via monorail and you get to the sand and look out to the ocean and all you see is oil tankers and factories spewing smoke on the horizon. It was like some sort of futuristic dystopia."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "80d4477a-62c7-4d25-a28b-467bb3c45220",
                                                "sense_id": "5c8c9436-d3b5-3653-9bbd-05539e579350",
                                                "text": "Like many dystopiae, it ends up with the lead characters wandering into a sylvan wilderness (an English wood) away from the nasty city."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "50700f52-964c-3cdc-a27c-cab8c8d1335a",
                                        "entry_id": "adcda362-e596-3b26-a5ab-9071d666c8a8",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Anatomical tissue that is not found in its usual place."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0b362389-52ef-429c-b2ef-90a7f7ab2f3b",
                                                "sense_id": "50700f52-964c-3cdc-a27c-cab8c8d1335a",
                                                "text": "The patient suffers from adrenal dystopia."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b95fc394-a064-4a89-84d7-648fbefb5282",
                                                "sense_id": "50700f52-964c-3cdc-a27c-cab8c8d1335a",
                                                "text": "2. Dystopiæ of separate organs."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "97a5b061-4064-45dc-8c1d-1d9008a29281",
                                                "sense_id": "50700f52-964c-3cdc-a27c-cab8c8d1335a",
                                                "text": "Davis, J. E. (J. Urol., Vol. xx-155, 1928), writing on the surgical pathology of malformation in the kidneys and ureters, classifies these anomalies into three groups: (a) anomalies of position (dystopiae)."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "723f5160-3022-4319-b658-239413d02f38",
                                                "sense_id": "50700f52-964c-3cdc-a27c-cab8c8d1335a",
                                                "text": "While harelips were mostly associated with dystopiae of the incisor teeth, median jaw fusion was accompanied by characteristic dental fusion."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "bfb5eb0c-4912-484b-8a4d-53626e450e43",
